Usages:
  conventions: .goga/usages/conventions.md
  json: |
    Python stdlib json module. Use json.loads for parsing LLM responses and
    catch json.JSONDecodeError to wrap into a domain error. Defensive parsing only —
    never trust LLM output structure without schema validation.

Annotations: |
  Application-layer use-case: bring local specs to the remote state by the saved configuration
  and rebuild the traceability graph conditionally, as one transaction. The remote clone is
  the source of truth — local spec edits are overwritten silently. Any failure leaves the
  specs and the graph exactly as before the attempt.

  Branch priority, checked top to bottom:
  - Empty file diff — nothing is touched, no LLM, the graph stays as is (or stays missing).
  - Removals-only diff — when a graph file exists it is pruned deterministically, no LLM;
    when it does not, the specs are applied and the graph stays missing — still no LLM (AC8).
  - Added/updated present — LLM credentials are validated BEFORE specs are applied; the graph
    is revised incrementally when it exists, or built from scratch via `run_discover` when
    it does not.

  Removed endpoints are reconciled against the post-update tree — an endpoint still declared
  by any surviving, modified, or added spec is never pruned from the graph.

  LLM responses are parsed defensively (provider-agnostic): strip prose/code fences around
  the JSON payload; wrap json.JSONDecodeError into `LLMResponseParseError` with a raw payload
  excerpt; validate the shape dict[str, list[str]]; drop sources and targets outside the
  endpoint universe.

  Domain exceptions propagate uncaught with one exception: the rebuild block is the single
  rollback point — its failures are wrapped into `GraphRebuildFailedError` after
  `staged_specs_swap` has restored the specs. Raw `load_config` failures propagate unwrapped.
  The CLI handler maps domain errors to user-facing errors.
  Logs INFO at start/end, DEBUG for intermediate steps; SWAX_LLM_TOKEN never in logs.

  Use `conventions` for code writing rules and testing.
  Use `project-config` for `load_config` and the `Config` shape.
  Use `environment` for `require_vars` (lazy LLM credential validation before applying specs).
  Use `specs-repository` for `clone_specs`, `RepositoryCloneError`, and `SpecsNotFoundError`.
  Use `spec-mirroring` for `compare_specs`, `staged_specs_swap`, `validate_specs_location`.
  Use `project-layout` for `copy_specs` (staging assembly).
  Use `parsing` for `discover_specs`, `parse_spec`, and `SpecParseError`.
  Use `diff` for `diff_specs`, `classify_endpoint_changes`, and `EndpointDiff`.
  Use `extraction` for `extract_paths` and `extract_schemas`.
  Use `graph-lifecycle` for `TraceabilityGraph`, `load_traceability`, `save_traceability`.
  Use `incremental-graph-prompts` for prompt assembly.
  Use `llm-transport` for `build_llm_client`, `LLMClient`, `LLMCallError`, `LLMRateLimitedError`,
  and `LLMResponseParseError`.
  Use `json` for defensive parsing of the LLM JSON response.

