"run_update(project_root: pathlib.Path) -> output: str":
  location: run_update.py
  annotations: |
    Use-case "update": mirror local specs to the remote state by the saved configuration and
    rebuild the traceability graph conditionally — non-interactive, transactional.

    `project_root`: root of the Swax project — .swax/config.yml describes the source and
      layout, .swax/traceability.yml is updated according to the diff.
    `output`: the terminal summary text (change groups plus graph status line) for the CLI
      handler to echo.

    Algorithm:
    1. Read `Config` via `load_config` — raw failures propagate unwrapped.
    2. Guard the target via `validate_specs_location`.
    3. Inside `clone_specs`: classify differences via `compare_specs`.
    4. Empty diff — return the up-to-date message; specs and graph stay untouched, the LLM is
       never required (even when no graph exists).
    5. Collect endpoints of removed spec files (membership in `discover_specs` over the local
       root) from their last local content via `parse_spec` + `extract_paths`, reconciled
       against the endpoints still declared by surviving remote specs — only endpoints absent
       from the whole post-update tree are pruned, in both branches.
    6. Otherwise — validate LLM credentials via `require_vars` BEFORE any mutation; when the
       graph file exists, prepare the incremental input: endpoint diff per modified spec-file
       pair via `diff_specs` + `classify_endpoint_changes` (merged), with the collected endpoints
       of removed spec files folded into the merged removed list — whole-file removals are pruned
       in the same revision; endpoints and schemas of
       added spec files via `extract_paths` + `extract_schemas` — spec files are filtered by
       membership in `discover_specs` over the local root and the clone; non-spec files are
       mirrored silently but never parsed.
    7. Assemble staging next to the target via `copy_specs` from the clone; a stale staging
       leftover from a crashed run is removed first.
    8. Inside `staged_specs_swap`:
       a. Removals-only — when the graph file exists: load the graph via `load_traceability`,
          drop removed endpoints via `TraceabilityGraph` remove_paths, deduplicate, persist
          via `save_traceability_atomically`; graph status "rebuilt". When it does not exist:
          skip graph work entirely — the graph stays missing and no status line is rendered.
       b. Incremental — build prompts via `build_incremental_graph_system_prompt` /
          `build_incremental_graph_user_prompt`, ask via `LLMClient` built by
          `build_llm_client`, parse defensively, filter to the endpoint universe, construct
          the `TraceabilityGraph`, deduplicate, persist via `save_traceability_atomically`;
          graph status "rebuilt".
       c. No graph file — delegate the full rebuild to `run_discover`; graph status "built".
    9. Wrap failures of the rebuild block into `GraphRebuildFailedError` — by that point
       `staged_specs_swap` has restored the previous specs state; when no graph file existed
       before the attempt and the failed build left one behind, remove
       .swax/traceability.yml first.
    10. Render the summary via `render_update_summary` and return it.

    Requirements:
    - The whole path is one transaction: on any failure the specs and the graph are exactly
      as before the attempt; a repeated run sees the same diff and converges.
    - A failed first build (no graph file before the attempt) leaves no graph file behind —
      a partially written .swax/traceability.yml is removed before the failure is wrapped.
    - LLM credentials are validated before specs are applied whenever the LLM branch is taken.
    - All network operations and staging assembly happen before the live directory is mutated.
    - The final graph write is atomic — the previous graph survives a failed write.
    - SWAX_LLM_TOKEN never appears in logs or the returned output.

    Constraints:
    - Do not catch domain exceptions except inside the rebuild block (the rollback point);
      never catch generic Exception.
    - Do not write anything outside the specs directory and .swax/traceability.yml — the
      config and the rest of the project stay untouched.
    - No interactive prompts — all inputs come from .swax/config.yml and the environment.

"GraphRebuildFailedError(reason: str)":
  location: errors.py
  annotations: |
    Raised by run_update when the graph rebuild after applying specs fails — the specs have
    been restored from the backup at that point.

    `reason`: short diagnostic reason carried from the underlying failure.

"save_traceability_atomically(graph: TraceabilityGraph, path: pathlib.Path)":
  location: save_traceability_atomically.py
  annotations: |
    Persists the graph so the previous file survives a failed write.

    `graph`: graph to write, deduplicated by the caller.
    `path`: destination file path (.swax/traceability.yml).

    Algorithm:
    1. Serialize via `save_traceability` into a temporary file next to `path`.
    2. Replace `path` with the temporary file in a single rename.
    3. On any failure, remove the temporary file and let the error propagate.

    Requirements:
    - The destination is replaced in one uninterrupted operation — no partially written target.
    - The temporary file lives next to the destination.

    Constraints:
    - Serialization semantics stay with `save_traceability` — no format changes.

"render_update_summary(changes: SpecsChanges, graph_status: str | None) -> output: str":
  location: render_update_summary.py
  annotations: |
    Renders the terminal summary of an applied update.

    `changes`: the applied file classification (paths relative to the specs location).
    `graph_status`: "rebuilt" for incremental/prune updates, "built" for a from-scratch
      build, None (omitted) when no graph existed and none was produced — the graph status
      line is skipped.
    `output`: the Added:, Updated:, and Removed: groups (sorted relative paths, empty groups
      omitted) followed by the graph status line when `graph_status` is not None.

    Requirements:
    - English output; paths relative to the specs location; sorted alphabetically within groups.
    - Empty groups are not printed.

    Constraints:
    - Pure transformation — no I/O, no LLM calls.

